Part of the finding is wrong, part stands, and the design already carries all of the content in prose, so I downgrade rather than refute.

What the finding gets wrong:
1. "shows the ordinary order but not the invariant": diagram 3's boxes literally read "Append even token: Write even row. Zero odd partner." and "Append odd token: Preserve even row. Write odd row." [design-new-tensor-type.html:274-283]. That IS the invariant of Note [Zero-Initialized V Slots] ("every even-indexed V write zeroes the odd partner") [kv_cache.hpp@f46e48bab498:2055-2065]. The diagram names the rule; it omits only the corner cases.
2. "the invariant the design calls trickiest": the design never uses "trickiest" or any equivalent; the section is titled "V padding and write ordering" [design-text.txt:373]. Unsupported attribution.
3. "Drop or shrink diagrams 1 and 2": jhan's standing rule says "use diagrams to capture hierarchy and sequencing" [~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, "Present data and concepts visually by default"]. Diagram 1 (owner -> view -> kernel access chain) and diagram 2 (main -> parent -> child PR order) are exactly hierarchy/sequencing diagrams. The finding gives no evidence they mislead; redundancy with the adjacent table is not a defect under the user's rule.

What the design already states in prose (every element the finding wants drawn):
- odd count -> weighted sum reads the unused row -> must be zero -> NaN*0 [design-text.txt:375];
- stale pair reuse: "Writing an even token clears the following odd token's row whenever the pair is reused" [375]; tests must "Include reused storage and all even/odd append combinations" [518];
- which writers zero: set_v(even) [392], append even destination [440], set_pair even form [330];
- which must preserve: row.set(expr) "Preserve the other row in its stored pair" [262], "Ordinary row assignment must preserve the other row in the pair. This applies even when the odd row is written first." [392], odd append [332, 394];
- uninitialized-pair read hazard [430].
An implementing agent reading this page would not guess wrong. The design does not defer a figure, so I cannot refute the presentation gap outright, but no guidance changes, which puts it at "note" (worth knowing, no change required), not "minor".

Verified source facts behind the finding's evidence: append_v even-destination paths store [0 b] / [0 a] [kv_cache.hpp@f46e48bab498:1866-1920]; set_v even path zero-extends via _mm512_cvtepu16_epi32 [2289-2305]; zero <svg> in the HTML (grep count 0).

The finding's central premise is false in the primary source. Ben's straw-man (conversation comment 5765866077, section "3. Storage is an aligned array", verbatim in /home/jhan/workspace/intel-AMX/VNNIed-K-in-place/issue4525/evidence/github-review.json) already writes all four overloads:
  auto as_view() & noexcept {...}
  auto as_view() const & noexcept {...}
  auto as_view() && = delete;
  auto as_view() const && = delete;
So "Ben's straw-man ... list[s] only &, const& and && = delete" is wrong. The finding appears to have relied on the lead's abbreviation in facts.md ("as_view() & / const& / && = delete"), which drops the `const &&` line; the primary source contradicts that abbreviation and I am flagging it explicitly.

The claim about the design is also not what the text says. The operations-table row (design-text.txt line 268) reads "Delete overloads for rvalue owners (temporary values or objects marked for moving)" - plural "overloads", with no enumeration at all; it does not "list only &, const& and && = delete". The only place the design writes `as_view() &&` (line 290, HTML line 207: "Deleting only as_view() && is insufficient") is in the slice paragraph, and its point is that slicing an rvalue owner needs its own guard because the existing general slice takes a const reference (verified: tensor/slice.hpp:26-127@f46e48bab498 takes `const_views<B,...> const&`). It is not a statement that the design intends only `&&` to be deleted. The design also points the implementer to Ben's example under "#3: Claude's example and Ben's concern" (line 80-82), which carries the four lines.

The C++ mechanics in the finding are correct and I do not dispute them: [over.load]/2 forbids mixing ref-qualified and unqualified overloads of the same name and parameter-type-list; a const rvalue would bind to `const&` if `const&&` were not deleted; ref-qualifiers on ordinary members do not affect std::is_trivially_copyable; and h/tron/tensor/tensor.hpp:273-274@f46e48bab498 (`std::construct_at(&data[i], rhs[i]);`) constructs `element = tensor<d1, ds...>` rows, so there is no interaction with the owner. But those correct facts do not rescue the finding, because the omission it alleges does not exist in Ben's text, and the all-or-none rule is a hard compile error, so an implementer who forgot it could not build a wrong binary.
